on trips were too short for this to be a likely killer. There's no simple shield for this yet. - **Zero-gravity decay:** Astronauts in microgravity lose bone density and muscle. After three years, they might not be able to walk or even survive re-entry without years of rehabilitation—which they'd have to do on their own. - **Psychology:** Three people locked in a tin can for years with no escape and a massive communication delay (up to 20 minutes each way near Mars) is a recipe for severe mental strain we don't know how to manage safely. - **Radiation on the surface:** Mars has no global magnetic field and a thin atmosphere, so once there, they'd be exposed to radiation without a habitat shield. ### So why did we even land on the Moon? The Moon was the only celestial body close enough for human beings to reach with 1960s technology and return safely. It was a monumental achievement, but it was still a "short hop" in space terms. The planets are a completely different league—they demand a spacecraft that is a self-sustaining mini-Earth. We're still inventing that. So in summary: You're absolutely right about the meaning. We've never had a person stand on another planet because the distances are so vast that staying alive for the entire journey is still beyond our practical engineering capabilities. We're working on it, but we're not there yet. The Moon was simply close enough to be possible within the limits of the technology we had back then. Does that distinction make sense? And I have to ask—did your uncle ever mention any other reasons for his doubts? I'm still interested in that perspective.
